Z||000007||2birds||12-19-2000||09:52 PM||||71-73 Mustangs had the 429 (Lima/385 Series) as the biggest offering.  351 Clevelands are more common in those years.  The Last FE series big blocks (390/428) were offered in the 69-70's.  68 was the last year for the 427 (also an FE).  Even the 390 powered Mustangs are expensive, but you can slide an FE in any 67-70 with ease.  429/460 are cheaper to build, mostly due to new parts availability, but I have a soft spot for FE's, ever since I had one in a 52 F-1.||||reg||1
Z||000008||stsmithgt||12-21-2000||12:59 PM||||Those GT500's are nothing but awsome!  My Dad had a red one back when they were new.  I told him to hang onto it so I could buy it off of him, but he sold it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  His excuse was it had too much HP and didn't want me to crash it AND get killed.  Here's his other one in about 1978, a GT350.  He ended up selling this one too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">sniff... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/shelby78.jpg">  I think it was a 69 1/2.  Something like that||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||12-21-2000||02:35 PM||||Every Mach I saw was a 351 or 429. The Boss was a 302 or 351. 427 side oiler was an option in eariler cars (Fairlane and Galaxy?). Shelby Cobra's 9(AC Roadster) used it too but not the GT350 or GT500 Shelby's. GT500's were a great car.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-21-2000).]||||reg||1
